+++ b/docs/tee-soft-rkp-apex.md
@@ -0,0 +1,186 @@
+# TEE Soft RKP APEX
+
+## Scope and current state (2026-11-14)
+
+The on-demand issuer is packaged as the independent, updatable APEX
+`org.system.tee.soft.rkp`. The initial version is 1 and must be preinstalled in
+the ROM. The executable path is
+`/apex/org.system.tee.soft.rkp/bin/tee_soft_rkp_issuer`.
+New package, certificate and Soong module identifiers use neutral names.
+Existing source provenance notices are retained.
+
+This migration changes packaging and dependency availability. The issuance
+algorithm, version-1 socket protocol, Keybox installation path and ASTER gates
+remain as described in [tee-soft-rkp-issuer.md](tee-soft-rkp-issuer.md).
+Local component management is now implemented in the Advanced RKP page; see
+[tee-soft-rkp-component-update.md](tee-soft-rkp-component-update.md).
+It uses the existing privileged system APEX installation channel. No component
+downloader is included.
+
+Static review is complete for this patch; building, policy compilation and
+device validation are outstanding. `supportsRebootlessUpdate` declares the
+intended installation mode, not evidence of successful activation on Marble.
+
+## Package and dependency boundary
+
+- `native/rkp_issuer/apex/Android.bp` defines the APEX, its two signing modules,
+  APEX-only init configuration and the file-contexts source provider.
+- LineageParts requires the APEX rather than the standalone helper. The helper
+  is available only to this APEX, so its platform variant is not installed.
+  There is no second service definition or system_ext executable installation.
+- Only the device's primary ABI is packaged. The minimum SDK is 35, a finalized
+  API level supported by this source tree; this is not a cross-version promise
+  for older ROMs.
+- curl, cppbor, jsoncpp and the frozen RKP V3 NDK interface are linked into the
+  helper. Only the legacy V1 RPC is used. `libcutils_sockets` supplies the init
+  control-socket API without pulling in the full libcutils runtime library.
+- Private dynamic dependencies (including BoringSSL, libbase and libc++) are
+  collected by Soong into the APEX. Stable system interfaces such as
+  libbinder_ndk, liblog and Bionic remain stub-linked external dependencies.
+  The actual ELF dependency closure and generated linker namespace must still
+  be checked after a build. System CA certificates remain an intentional
+  external data dependency at `/system/etc/security/cacerts`.
+- Availability is extended only for this APEX in curl, cppbor, the RKP NDK
+  backend, libcrypto, libssl and BoringSSL's bcm_object. Existing platform
+  availability is retained. No source-library fork is introduced.
+- The APEX has no Java/JNI contents, exported native libraries, persistent
+  daemon or module data directory. Its manifest must continue to have empty
+  provideNativeLibs/jniLibs after Soong processing for non-staged installation.
+
+## Signing and release integration
+
+The payload AVB key and outer APK certificate are independent of each other,
+the platform key and the ASTER keys. The checked-in files under
+`native/rkp_issuer/apex/` are **public development signing material**:
+
+- `org.system.tee.soft.rkp.pem` and `.avbpubkey`: payload development key pair.
+- `org.system.tee.soft.rkp.pk8` and `.x509.pem`: container development key pair.
+
+Their private keys are deliberately public, as with AOSP development keys.
+A ROM released with these trust anchors accepts updates signed with them;
+replace both before releasing a trusted production image. Production private
+keys belong in controlled release infrastructure outside public source trees.
+Keep the signing identity consistent between the preinstalled APEX and updates;
+switching development to production trust requires a corresponding ROM change.
+
+The standard target-files re-signing path supports both replacements:
+
+```sh
+sign_target_files_apks \
+  --extra_apks org.system.tee.soft.rkp.apex=/secure/rkp/container \
+  --extra_apex_payload_key org.system.tee.soft.rkp.apex=/secure/rkp/org.system.tee.soft.rkp.pem \
+  input-target_files.zip signed-target_files.zip
+```
+
+`/secure/rkp/container` denotes the release `.x509.pem`/`.pk8` prefix. This is a
+release-integration example, not an action performed during this migration.
+Audit the resulting `META/apexkeys.txt` and re-signed package. An independently
+distributed update must use those same production identities for BOTH layers.
+Do not distribute a fresh development-signed build as an update to a
+production-signed ROM.
+
+For direct source builds, AOSP's ApexKeyDir lookup can substitute payload
+`org.system.tee.soft.rkp.pem`/`.avbpubkey` from the configured default certificate
+directory. The container uses an explicit certificate module: override it with
+`PRODUCT_CERTIFICATE_OVERRIDES` pointing at a private `android_app_certificate`
+module, or re-sign target-files as above. Setting the default platform
+certificate alone does not replace the explicit container certificate, and
+missing payload override files can fall back to the checked-in development
+pair. Check both actual keys in the final release artifact.
+
+Increase the integer `version` in `apex_manifest.json` for each published update;
+keep versions monotonic across independent releases and subsequent ROM builds.
+Preserve the module name and socket contract. A protocol change that requires
+a different UI must be coordinated with a ROM update.
+
+## Init and SELinux
+
+The rc is installed solely at `/apex/org.system.tee.soft.rkp/etc/init.rc`.
+The existing service name `tee_soft_rkp_issuer`, system UID, system/inet groups,
+disabled/oneshot flags and restricted init-created socket are preserved.
+There is no auto-start action. Init discovers this service from the active APEX
+after normal APEX activation; it is not an early-boot bootstrap component.
+
+There is no platform service to override, so `override` and the init service
+option `updatable` are unnecessary. Init's APEX unload path finds services by
+their defining rc path, terminates them, removes the old definitions, and
+loads the newly active APEX configuration.
+
+The payload file_contexts labels the helper with the existing
+`tee_soft_rkp_issuer_exec` type. Its provider is registered in
+`system/sepolicy/apex/Android.bp` to meet Soong's location requirement.
+The obsolete system_ext binary label is removed; the socket label remains.
+The init domain transition and existing ROM policy continue to assign
+`tee_soft_rkp_issuer` authority. No new HAL, network, control-property or data
+write permission is added. Updated code receives this fixed authority by the
+trusted entry-point label, not by inheriting permissions from a downloaded
+file. New policy authority still requires a ROM update.
+
+## Idle update procedure and limits
+
+The integrated local updater serializes updates with the page's Keybox jobs.
+External privileged installers/operators must also serialize updates with issuance:
+
+1. Wait until the UI has finished issuance AND local Keybox installation/result
+   recording. Do not update with a pending/recoverable job or an active import
+   or network Keybox update. Keep the UI from starting a new operation during
+   installation.
+2. Confirm `getprop init.svc.tee_soft_rkp_issuer` reports `stopped`. A successful
+   signing reply alone is insufficient: the helper retains its result until
+   ACK or expiry. Do not stop it manually to force an update.
+3. Install the higher-version package using the existing privileged non-staged
+   APEX installer. The future authorized ADB validation command is
+   `adb install --apex --non-staged update.apex` (no `--staged` or
+   `--force-non-staged`). Plain `adb install update.apex` defaults to a staged
+   installation in this tree; the explicit `--non-staged` flag is required.
+4. Check the installation result and the active entry in
+   `/apex/apex-info-list.xml`: module name, versionCode, isActive and modulePath.
+   Inspect current state after failure too; do not infer activation from the
+   candidate package or PackageManager's stored/staged version alone.
+5. Reopen the page and start a new explicit issuance only after activation.
+   Verify that the new process's `/proc/<pid>/exe` resolves to the updated APEX,
+   that its label is unchanged, and that exactly one service/socket exists.
+
+External installation is not protected by the integrated updater's lock.
+APEX/init does not refuse installation just because this
+helper is busy: it terminates APEX services. Installing during a job can lose
+the result, including after the server has issued certificates. Existing
+recovery only queries; it must not silently issue another request.
+
+apexd attempts to reload the active configuration on installation failure.
+Validate the actual active version rather than claiming automatic rollback
+always succeeds. RKP server rejection, certificate expiry or firmware changes
+are issuance failures and never trigger an automatic component rollback.
+An update can change protocol handling; it cannot guarantee server acceptance
+or recover hardware behavior removed by a firmware update.
+
+## Verification ledger
+
+Static checks for this migration:
+
+- Parse/format all changed Soong files with bpfmt; parse APEX manifest JSON.
+- Cross-check package identity, required-module wiring, min SDK, APEX-only helper
+  availability, init path/options and file-contexts provider/entry-point label.
+- Review private dependencies and stable external interfaces against their
+  current definitions; review non-staged eligibility and service reload paths
+  in local apexd/init source.
+- Inspect signing files for valid key structure, matching public/private pairs
+  and distinct payload/container identities, without logging private material.
+- Check diffs and whitespace across all affected repositories.
+
+Still required in a separately authorized build/device iteration:
+
+- Soong dependency resolution, min-SDK/link checks, compiled SELinux policy and
+  APEX filesystem labels; inspect `apexkeys.txt`, manifest and ELF DT_NEEDED.
+- Fresh ROM boot: preinstalled APEX active, unique rc/service, no old standalone
+  install, unchanged socket ownership and helper domain/HAL/network access.
+- Higher-version signed update while idle without reboot; next helper process
+  uses the new mount and completes an explicit V1 issuance.
+- Wrong container key, wrong payload key and lower-version package rejected;
+  failed installation leaves a usable active version with visible diagnostics.
+- Busy/result-handoff behavior and no automatic retry after interruption.
+
+No build, device operation, signing request, policy compilation, staging or Git
+commit was performed as part of the static migration check. If device evidence
+requires changing the rebootless-update objective, stop for confirmation before
+changing the accepted design.
