@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
+# TEE Soft Emu on-device RKP issuer
+
+## Agreed scope
+
+V1 AIDL only. A separately labelled native helper is started on demand by init; it
+creates an exportable software P-256 private key and asks the real device RKP HAL
+for a production CSR, then obtains the certificate chain from the RKP server.
+This is an online acquisition path for EC-only Keybox material, not offline
+certificate issuance, a hardware-key export, or an insertion into the RKPD pool.
+Qualcomm CSR_V2/native V1 commands and fallback/retry issuance are out of scope.
+
+Existing Keystore2, PureSoft and ASTER generation gates are untouched. Receiving
+new material does not enable TEE Soft Emu or rewrite existing keys/certificates.
+
+## Components
+
+- `native/rkp_issuer`: C++17 binary in the `org.system.tee.soft.rkp` APEX,
+  generated RKP V3 NDK interface
+  (calling its legacy V1 method), cppbor, BoringSSL, curl and jsoncpp. Installed
+  through LineageParts' required APEX dependency. No runtime Java bridge.
+  Packaging, signing and update boundaries: [tee-soft-rkp-apex.md](tee-soft-rkp-apex.md).
+- init service `tee_soft_rkp_issuer`: disabled/oneshot, system UID, system/inet
+  groups, separate coredomain, no root or additional capabilities. Starts idle.
+- `/dev/socket/tee_soft_rkp_issuer`: init-created stream socket, mode 0600,
+  system:system; separate pathname label and helper-domain socket object label.
+  Helper calls listen; no extra init listen permission is needed.
+- SELinux in device/lineage/sepolicy/common/private: exact start control property,
+  independently labelled/denied legacy ctl fallback, helper HAL/network grants,
+  system_app socket connection. The standard hal_keymint client group grants
+  both RKP and KeyMint service access to the helper, not to the application.
+- `RkpIssuerClient`: bounded socket protocol and progress queries.
+- `KeyboxUpdater`: shared import/update/issuance exclusion, task recovery and
+  existing XML validation/atomic installation/rollback. The helper never opens
+  current Keybox/config files and has no new policy permitting their access.
+
+## V1 contract
+
+The zero-HBK HKDF-SHA256 MAC uses context `Key to MAC public keys` and a
+COSE_Mac0 for one software P-256 public key. It is firmware-specific behavior,
+not a property guaranteed by the AIDL interface. Marble V1 success was reported
+by the user; this implementation has not been executed against that device.
+
+A fresh fetchEekChain request supplies challenge and complete EEK chains. The
+helper selects the curve reported by getHardwareInfo. It calls
+`generateCertificateRequest(false, keys, eek, challenge, ...)` on /default.
+Opaque DeviceInfo and ProtectedData are checked for structure but inserted into
+the final CSR without decoding/re-encoding. The unverified DeviceInfo map is
+empty, matching Duck's optional-fingerprint behavior; fetchEekChain still sends
+the actual build fingerprint. Encrypted V1 evidence is not locally verified.
+
+The Google HTTPS endpoint follows Duck's protocol. TLS peer/hostname checks use
+/system/etc/security/cacerts. Redirects and proxy environment use are disabled.
+The server reply must yield exactly one certificate path. Its leaf public key
+must equal the generated key; the complete path is ordered, each link and the
+self-signed root are signature-checked with libcrypto. This checks internal
+consistency, not independent root trust, revocation or an integrity verdict.
+
+Private key PEM and certificates are assembled into EC-only XML. No private key,
+CSR, response body or XML is logged or saved by the helper. Result bytes cross
+the restricted socket; Java clears its byte array after installation. General
+allocator/string copies are not claimed to provide secure-memory erasure.
+
+## Protocol and lifecycle
+
+Each socket connection exchanges one big-endian uint32 length followed by UTF-8
+JSON. Version=1, lowercase 32-hex job ID, actions start/query/ack. Requests are
+limited to 1 KiB and replies to 128 KiB. Peer UID must be 1000 in both directions;
+this authorizes the trusted system UID, not a unique application package.
+
+The first accepted start owns the process. Repeating start for that ID only
+returns status; a different ID cannot trigger another issuance. Query never
+starts a job. Replies contain status, stage and numeric code, and XML only after
+success. UI tracks stages rather than claiming installed success before save.
+
+The job ID is persisted before service startup. Page recreation uses the
+process-wide worker; application-process recovery queries the stored ID only.
+If the service/result is gone, recovery fails rather than reissuing. Pending
+recovery is attempted once per job per application process. After installation
+or terminal failure Java records the result, clears the pending ID and sends
+ack. A local save failure never retries issuance. Abrupt death between material
+installation and application metadata/ack remains subject to the existing
+multi-file installation limitations.
+
+Helper has a 15-second initial request window, 30-second final result window,
+and an unconditional 180-second process watchdog covering blocked HAL calls.
+Client waits up to 150 seconds between bounded socket calls (individual calls
+can extend that budget), with a 5-second reconnect window. HTTP has 15-second
+connect/40-second total limits per request. No automatic network retry is used;
+a transport failure after submission may mean the server issued certificates
+whose response was lost. Watchdog exit or result expiry discards in-memory
+material. An already-active helper rejects a new job until it exits.
+
+CBOR responses are capped at 64 KiB and scanned before cppbor parsing (depth 16,
+128 entries/container, definite encodings). CSR limit is 0xa000; returned DER
+chain limit is 16 KiB/16 certificates; XML limit is 64 KiB. No disk handoff cache.
+
+## Evidence and verification (2026-11-13)
+
+Reference: /home/weiguangtwk/Duck-ToolBox db43a2d, V1 request/assembly, issuer
+bridge and certificate artifacts. Its MIT notices and rkp-v1-oracle b28480c
+attribution are included in native/rkp_issuer/NOTICE and Soong licenses.
+
+Static checks passed: tree-sitter parsing of one C++ and six Java source files;
+XML parsing of nine manifest/preference/locale files; resource references and
+RKP locale parity; protocol and init/policy/module name consistency; bpfmt
+parsing/formatting; and git diff --check in LineageParts and device/lineage/sepolicy.
+Relevant cppbor/BoringSSL/NDK/LocalSocket API declarations and HAL call argument
+order were checked against current source. SELinux macros/control-property
+fallback handling were reviewed against current source without compiling policy.
+No compilation, policy compilation, executable tests, network issuance or device
+operation is authorized/performed in this iteration. Static parsing does not
+establish successful linking, policy acceptance or firmware compatibility.
+
+## Runtime checks left for a future authorized build
+
+- Soong dependencies, generated NDK signatures, linking and SELinux neverallows.
+- Exact ctl start allowed; legacy start/stop/restart paths not granted to UI.
+- Socket ownership/labels, peer credentials, DNS/TLS and real HAL access.
+- Marble full V1 issuance, returned-key binding and subsequent Keystore2 use.
+- Local/import/update exclusion, lost start reply, recreation/recovery, same-ID
+  idempotence, wrong-ID rejection, helper timeout and no retry after submission.
+- Installation failure preserves old material; ACK loss/result expiry does not
+  create another issuance. UI must distinguish acquisition from installation.
+
+## Marble vendor declaration repair (2026-11-13)
+
+Observed LineageOS result: hardware/-2 and no registered RKP /default service.
+Stock comparison device 207313b8 (marble, OS3.0.4.0.VMRCNXM) exposes that service
+and declares it in android.hardware.security.keymint-service-qti-rkp.xml.
+Its init service configuration matches the legacy manifest variant in this tree.
+Stock and tree KeyMint binary/libqtikeymint .text and .rodata sections match;
+libkeymasterdeviceutils.so hashes match. Overall ELF hashes differ because of
+extraction adjustments, not evidence of different RKP implementation code.
+
+An initial report of missing RKP DT_NEEDED entries in the tree was incorrect.
+Re-running configured fixups and comparing against Git HEAD proved that both
+ELFs already contain exactly one android.hardware.security.rkp-V1-ndk.so entry
+and are byte-for-byte unchanged. No ELF patch or vendor replacement is required.
+
+Prepared repair:
+- vendor/xiaomi/sm8450-common/proprietary/vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/
+  android.hardware.security.keymint-service-qti.xml: add only the missing
+  android.hardware.security.keymint/IRemotelyProvisionedComponent/default HAL.
+- device/xiaomi/sm8450-common/extract-files.py: add an idempotent manifest fixup
+  for that existing destination/source-list path, retaining the existing ELF
+  dependency fixup. The stock -rkp filename is not substituted into the common
+  proprietary-files list; extracting directly from that stock layout still needs
+  the corresponding source filename mapping as a separate extraction input choice.
+
+Static checks passed: Python AST, XML HAL preservation and single RKP entry,
+configured fixup idempotence for XML and both ELFs, original ELF byte equality,
+DT_NEEDED uniqueness, and git diff --check in both affected repositories.
+No build, device file change, KeyMint restart or certificate issuance performed.
+Service registration and full issuance remain to be validated on a new build.
+
+## RPC URL correction (2026-11-13)
+
+Device reached challenge stage and returned HTTP 404. Source comparison with
+Duck rpc_url and AOSP ServerInterface confirmed an incorrect slash separator.
+Both methods now use /v1:fetchEekChain and /v1:signCertificates, retaining the
+challenge query parameter. C++ syntax and URL construction static checks passed.
+No network request, compilation or deployment performed for this correction.
